 a submission for presentation at the INTERACT 99 conference inEdinburgh, 30th August to 3rd September 1999. Accepted submissions will be published in the INTERACT 99 conferenceproceedings.\par\pard\plain \qj \f20\fs20 \par\pard\plain \s5\qj \f20\fs20 {\plain \b\f20 KEYWORDS}  style, guide, HCI,millennium, Edinburgh\par\pard \s5\qj \par\pard\plain \qj \f20\fs20 \sect \sectd\sbknone\linemod0\headery0\footery0\cols2\colsx576\endnhere \pard\plain \qj\f20\fs20 {\plain \b\f20  \sect }\sectd\sbknone\linemod0\headery0\footery0\cols2\colsx454\endnhere \pard\plain\s6\sb400\sa120 \b\f201. GUIDELINES FOR SUBMISSION\par\pard\plain \qj \f20\fs20 \par\pard\plain \s8\qj\fi180 \f20\fs20 These Guidelines should be read inconjunction with the Call for Participation (available on the INTERACT 99Web site at www.bcs.org.uk/hci/ i99) or from the INTERACT 99 ConferenceOffice.\par\pard\plain \s6\sb400\sa120 \b\f20 2. SCOPE AND PURPOSE\par\pard\plain \s8\qj\fi180 \f20\fs20 Papers should present new work relatedto one or more of the conference topics listed in the Call. Full papersmust not exceed 8 printed pages. Papers for the professional practice and experience track should notexceed 6 pages. Posters and submissions for the doctoral consortium shouldnot exceed 2 pages. All submissions must represent original contributions,and be substantially differentfrom any paper the authors have published previously, or submitted forpublication elsewhere. \par\pard\plain \s6\sb400\sa120 \b\f20 3. FORMAT, STYLE AND CONTENT\par\pard\plain \s7\qj\sb240\sa120\keepn \b\f20 3.1 Page size\par\pard\plain \s8\qj\fi180 \f20\fs20 All material on each page should fitwithin a print area of 225 by 160 mm, positioned 20mm from the top of thepage, and 10 mm from the left margin, centred on the page. The target page sizefor the printed proceedings is 260 by 190 mm. A larger page size, up to A4,may be used for the submission \emdash  there will be no reduction orenlargement in the final printing.\par\pard \s8\qj\fi180 \par\pard \s8\qj\fi180 Text should be in double column format (except for thetitle and abstract \emdash see below) with a space of 10 mm between two 75 mm columns. Submissionsshould be prepared on a typesetter or word processor, and printed on alaser-quality or near-laser-quality printer \par\pard\plain \s7\qj\sb240\sa120\keepn \b\f20 3.2 Abstract and Keywords\par\pard\plain \s8\qj\fi180 \f20\fs20Every submission should begin with an abstract of not more than 150 words,followed by a set of keywords. The abstract and keywords should be singlecolumn, across the whole width of the paper. The abstract should be aconcise statement of the problem, approach, findings, and conclusions of the work described. \par\pard\plain \s7\qj\sb240\sa120\keepn \b\f20 3.3 Title and Authors\par\pard\plain \s8\qj\fi180 \f20\fs20 The title should be in Times Roman 18point, centred, as a single column across the page. The first line of thetitle should be at least 4 cm (1.5") from the top of the page. Leave twoblank lines after the title. \par\pard \s8\qj\fi180 \par\pard \s8\qj\fi180For initial submission the author information should only appear on thecover sheet (see below), and a corresponding amount of blank space left onthe main paper. In the final submission, author names should follow thetitle in 14 point Times Roman, single column, followed by one blank line. The author(s) affiliations(s) shouldfollow in the same format, followed (optionally) by email addresses. \par\pard\plain \s7\qj\sb240\sa120\keepn \b\f20 3.4 Normal or Body Text\par\pard\plain \s8\qj\fi180 \f20\fs20 Please use a 10-pointTimes Roman font, or other Roman font with serifs, as close as possible inappearance to Times Roman. Sans-serif or non-proportional fonts should beused only for special purposes, such as distinguishing source code text.(The Press 10-point font available to users of Script is a good substitute for Times Roman.) If Times Romanis not available, try the font named Computer Modern Roman. On a Macintosh,use the font named Times. \par\pard\plain \s7\qj\sb240\sa120\keepn \b\f20 3.5 First Page CopyrightNotice\par\pard\plain \s8\qj\fi180 \f20\fs20 Leave 2.5 cm (1") of blank space at thebottom of the first page for the copyright notice. For pages after thefirst page, continue to the bottom of the print area. Right margins shouldbe justified, not ragged. \par\pard\plain \s7\qj\sb240\sa120\keepn \b\f20 3.6 References and Citations\par\pard\plain \s8\qj\fi180 \f20\fs20 Use the Harvard system if possible.References should be cited in the text by name and date, for example(Apperley 1989) or (Benbow 1980, Papert 1980). A list of cited referencesis  included at the end as a separate un\endash numbered section, in alphabetical order of author name, and thenchronologically within author name. \par\pard \s8\qj\fi180 \par\pard \s8\qj\fi180References should be published materials accessible to the public. Internaltechnical reports may be cited only if they are easily accessible (i.e. youcan give the address to obtain the report within your citation) and may beobtained by any reader. Proprietary in\-formation may not be cited. Private communications should beacknowledged, not referenced (e.g., "[Robertson, personal communication]").\par\pard \s8\qj\fi180 \par\pard \s8\qj\fi180 Papers should not normally have more than 15 references.\par\pard\plain \s7\qj\sb240\sa120\keepn \b\f20 3.7 Page Numbering, Headers andFooters\par\pard\plain \s8\qj\fi180 \f20\fs20 Do not include headers, footers or pagenumbers in your submission. These will be added when the publications areassembled. \par\pard\plain \s7\qj\sb240\sa120\keepn \b\f20 3.8 Headings\par\pard\plain \s8\qj\fi180 \f20\fs20 First order headings should be in 12point bold upper case, left justified, preceded by two blank lines. Firstorder headings should be numbered consecutively. \par\pard \s8\qj\fi180 \par\pard \s8\qj\fi180 Second order headings should be in 12 point bold, mixedcase (i.e. upper case for first letter of word only). second order headingsshould be numbered consecutively, using a hierarchical system. \par\pard \s8\qj\fi180 \par\pard \s8\qj\fi180 The first line of each paragraph should start with threespaces. Lower level subsections should be avoided if possible. \par\pard\plain \s7\qj\sb240\sa120\keepn \b\f20 3.9 Figures\par\pard\plain \s8\qj\fi180 \f20\fs20Figures should be inserted at the appropriate point in your text. Figuresmay extend over the two columns up to 14.8 cm (5.8") if necessary. Blackand white photographs (not Polaroid prints) may be mounted on thecamera-ready paper with glue or double-sided tape. To avoid smudges, attach figures by paste or tape applied to theirback surfaces only. Each figure should have a figure caption in Times Roman(10 point). \par\pard \s8\qj\fi180 \par\pard \s8\qj\fi180 Colour figures cannot be accommodated in the printedproceedings. Papers should not normally have more than 4 figures. \par\pard\plain \s7\qj\sb240\sa120\keepn \b\f20 3.10 Language, Style andContent\par\pard\plain \s8\qj\fi180 \f20\fs20Papers should report original and recent work and must not be submitted toanother conference or journal. The written and spoken language of INTERACTis English. Spelling and punctuation may consistently use any dialect ofEnglish (e.g., British, Canadianor US). Hyphenation is optional. Please write for an internationalaudience: \par\pard \s8\qj\fi180 \par\pard \s8\qj\fi180 Write in a straightforward style. Use simple sentencestructure. Try to avoid long and complex sentence structures. Use commonand basic vocabulary (e.g., use the word "unusual" rather than the word"arcane").\par\pard \s8\qj\fi180 \par\pard \s8\qj\fi180 Briefly define or explain all technical terms. Explainall acronyms the first time they are used in your text e.g., "World WideWeb (WWW)".\par\pard \s8\qj\fi180 \par\pard \s8\qj\fi180 Explain local references (e.g., not everyone knows allcity names in a particular country). Explain "insider" comments. Ensurethat your whole audience understands any reference whose meaning you do not describe (e.g., do not assumethat everyone has used a Macintosh, or a particular application). Explaincolloquial language and puns. Understanding phrases like "red herring"requires a cultural knowledge of English. Humour and irony are often difficult to translate. Use unambiguousforms for culturally localised concepts, such as: times, dates, currenciesand numbers.\par\pard \s8\qj\fi180 \par\pard \s8\qj\fi180 Use of gender-specific pronouns (he, she) and othergendered words (chairman, manpower, man-months) should be avoided wherever possible. Rather use inclusive language(e.g., they, she or he, chair, staff, staff-hours, person-years) that isgender-neutral. \par\pard\plain \s7\qj\sb240\sa120\keepn \b\f20 3.11 Template file\par\pard\plain \s8\qj\fi180 \f20\fs20 This document is an example of therecommended format. It defines styles for {\i Title,} {\i Author, Address}lines, {\i Abstract} (and keywords), major headings ({\i Heading 1}), minorheadings ({\i Heading 2}), {\iBody }text, {\i Bullet} lists within body text, amd entries in the {\iRefernece} list. ({\i Normal} is the same as {\i Body} without the leadingindent\emdash it is not used.) There is an extra single-column section containing white space afterthe references, to force balancing of the columns on the final page.\par\pard\plain \s6\sb400\sa120 \b\f20 4 SUBMISSION PROCEDURE\par\pard\plain \s8\qj\fi180 \f20\fs20 Six copies of the printed paper shouldbe sent, to arrive not later than 25th January 1999, to: \par\pard \s8\qj\fi180 \parDr M. Angela Sasse,\parIPC Co-Chair, INTERACT '99,\parDept of Computer Science,\parUniversity College London\parGower St\parLondon WC1E 6BT\parUnited Kingdom\paremail: a.sasse@cs.ucl.ac.uk\par\par\pard \s8\qj\fi180 To aid anonymous reviewing, names and affiliations ofthe authors should be omitted from the main text of the paper. \par\pard \s8\qj\fi180 \par\pard \s8\qj\fi180 A separate cover sheet must be attached to each copy ofthe paper giving: \par\pard \s8\qj\fi180 \par\pard\plain \s9\qj\fi-300\li500 \f20\fs20 \bullet \tab paper title\par\bullet \tab names and affiliations of all authors\par\pard \s9\qj\fi-300\li500 \bullet \tab full address, email, telephone andfax numbers for the designated \par\bullet \tab author to whom correspondence about the paper should beaddressed keywords\par\pard \s9\qj\fi-300\li500 \bullet \tab abstract\par\pard\plain \s8\qj\fi180 \f20\fs20 \par\pard \s8\qj\fi180 This information should also be sent electronically tothe IPC co-chair (e-mail template and web form will be available here from16 November 1998). \par\pard \s8\qj\fi180 \par\pard \s8\qj\fi180 To speed up the reviewing process, we also encourageauthors to make an HTML version of their submission available. Details ofthe procedure for submitting an HTML version will be available here fromthe 16th of November. \par\pard \s8\qj\fi180 \par\pard \s8\qj\fi180 Faxed submissions cannot be accepted. Acknowledgement ofreceipt of submissions will be sent by e-mail within 7 days. \par\pard\plain \s6\sb400\sa120 \b\f20 5. REVIEW PROCEDURE\par\pard\plain \s8\qj\fi180 \f20\fs20 Papers will be reviewed anonymously byat least 4 members of an international panel of expert HCI researchers andpractitioners. \par\pard \s8\qj\fi180 \par\pard \s8\qj\fi180 Authors of full papers and doctoral consortium papers, and proposers of tutorials, will be informed of the outcome of thereview process by Thursday 1st April. For full and doctoral papers, thefinal version, incorporating any changes recommended by the reviewers, mustbe returned to the IPC Co-Chair or Doctoral Consortium co-chair by Monday 17th May to ensure inclusion in theproceedings. \par\pard \s8\qj\fi180 \par\pard \s8\qj\fi180 For other submission catgories, authors will be notifiedby 7th June.\par\pard \s8\qj\fi180 \par\pard \s8\qj\fi180 At least one author of each accepted paper must registerfor INTERACT 99 by the end of June,. otherwise the paper will not beincluded in the conference proceedings. \par\pard\plain \s6\sb400\sa120 \b\f20 REFERENCES\par\pard\plain \s10\qj\fi-180\li180 \f20\fs20 Apperley, M. D. and Spence, R.({1989}). \ldblquote Lean cuisine : a low-fat notation for menus.\rdblquote{\i Interacting with Computers} {\b 1}(1), {43-68}.\parBenbow, C.P. and Stanley, J.C. (1980) Sex Differences in mathematicalability: fact or artifact. {\i Science}, {\b 210}, 1262-4.\par\pard\plain \qj\fi-90\li90 \f20\fs20 Papert, S. (1980) Mindstorms. {\iChildren, computers, and powerful ideas.} Basic Books, New York.\par\pard\plain \s8\qj\fi180 \f20\fs20 \par\pard\plain \qj \f20\fs20 \sect \sectd\sbknone\linemod0\headery0\footery0\cols1\colsx454\endnhere \pard\plain\s5\qj \f20\fs20     \par\pard\plain \qj \f20\fs20 {\v \par}}